# Phenomena testsuite: entailment (1), contradiction (-1) and neutral (0) in about equal numbers,
# for quantifiers and monotonicity, negation, comparatives and equatives, relative clauses,
# presupposition, active/passive and dative alternation, plus anaphora, conditionals, a few
# ambiguous premises and some longer sentences. Built for the lfgxdrt inference grammar
# (no clausal embedding: attitude reports are not implemented).
#
# Checked 2026-11-02: every sentence parses (native XLE) and derives at least one reading (GSWB
# /deduce, meaning postulates + degree rules, no TAM rules). The regression view numbers the items
# n0-n149 in file order. Naturalness: tests/probes/testsuite_perplexity.py.
#
# Item header: # <id>: <phenomena> -- <source>
#   source: "after <reference>" for an item adapted from the literature, "new" otherwise.
#   [reading: ...] marks an item of an ambiguity group: its gold label holds under that reading,
#   and every reading of the group's premise is needed by at least one item.
# References: FraCaS = Cooper et al. (1996), The FraCaS test suite; K&R = Kamp & Reyle (1993),
#   From Discourse to Logic; Geach (1962), Reference and Generality; van der Sandt (1992),
#   Presupposition projection as anaphora resolution; Karttunen (1973), Presuppositions of
#   compound sentences; Levin (1993), English Verb Classes and Alternations; Yanaka et al. (2019),
#   Can neural networks understand monotonicity reasoning? (MED); May (1985), Logical Form.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Quantifiers and monotonicity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# q01: universal, relative clause -- after FraCaS 3.x (generalized quantifiers)
{
Every student who read the article passed the exam.
Ann is a student who read the article.
====
Ann passed the exam.
>>> 1
}

# q02: universal, restrictor not met -- new
{
Every student who read the article passed the exam.
Ann is a student.
====
Ann passed the exam.
>>> 0
}

# q03: universal, instance negated -- new
{
Every student who read the article passed the exam.
Ann is a student who read the article.
====
Ann did not pass the exam.
>>> -1
}

# q04: every, downward monotone restrictor -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
Every student who read a book passed the exam.
====
Every student who read a long book passed the exam.
>>> 1
}

# q05: every, restrictor is not upward monotone -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
Every student who read a long book passed the exam.
====
Every student who read a book passed the exam.
>>> 0
}

# q06: every, upward monotone scope -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
Every dog in the garden found a big bone.
====
Every dog in the garden found a bone.
>>> 1
}

# q07: no, downward monotone scope -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
No student read a book.
====
No student read a long book.
>>> 1
}

# q08: no, scope is not upward monotone -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
No student read a long book.
====
No student read a book.
>>> 0
}

# q09: no vs. some -- new
{
No student read a book.
====
Some student read a long book.
>>> -1
}

# q10: some vs. no -- new
{
Some student read a long book.
====
No student read a book.
>>> -1
}

# q11: some, not downward monotone -- new
{
A student read a book.
====
A student read a long book.
>>> 0
}

# q12: nobody vs. somebody -- new
{
Nobody complained.
====
Somebody complained.
>>> -1
}

# q13: each, instance -- after FraCaS 3.x (generalized quantifiers)
{
Each candidate received an offer.
Sue is a candidate.
====
Sue received an offer.
>>> 1
}

# q14: indefinite, no instance -- new
{
A candidate received an offer.
Sue is a candidate.
====
Sue received an offer.
>>> 0
}

# q15: no, instance -- new
{
No candidate received an offer.
Sue is a candidate.
====
Sue received an offer.
>>> -1
}

# q16: not every -- new
{
Not every student passed the exam.
Ann is a student.
====
Ann passed the exam.
>>> 0
}

# q17: not every vs. every -- new
{
Not every student passed the exam.
====
Every student passed the exam.
>>> -1
}

# q18: not every, some ... not -- new
{
Not every student passed the exam.
====
Some student did not pass the exam.
>>> 1
}

# q19: universal object, relative clause -- new
{
Kim read every book that Sue recommended.
Sue recommended Emma.
Emma is a book.
====
Kim read Emma.
>>> 1
}

# q20: universal object, restrictor not met -- new
{
Kim read every book that Sue recommended.
Emma is a book.
====
Kim read Emma.
>>> 0
}

# q21: universal object, instance negated -- new
{
Kim read every book that Sue recommended.
Sue recommended Emma.
Emma is a book.
====
Kim did not read Emma.
>>> -1
}

# q22: donkey sentence -- after Geach (1962), K&R (1993)
{
Every farmer who owns a donkey beats it.
Pedro is a farmer who owns a donkey.
====
Pedro beats a donkey.
>>> 1
}

# q23: donkey sentence, restrictor not met -- after Geach (1962), K&R (1993)
{
Every farmer who owns a donkey beats it.
Pedro is a farmer.
====
Pedro beats a donkey.
>>> 0
}

# q24: donkey sentence vs. no -- after Geach (1962), K&R (1993)
{
Every farmer who owns a donkey beats it.
Pedro is a farmer who owns a donkey.
====
No farmer beats a donkey.
>>> -1
}

# q25: every has no existential import -- after FraCaS 3.x (generalized quantifiers)
{
Every student who failed the exam complained.
====
A student failed the exam.
>>> 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Negation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# n01: sentential negation -- new
{
Kim did not pass the exam.
====
Kim passed the exam.
>>> -1
}

# n02: negation, downward monotone object -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
Kim did not read a book.
====
Kim did not read a long book.
>>> 1
}

# n03: negation, object not upward monotone -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
Kim did not read a long book.
====
Kim did not read a book.
>>> 0
}

# n04: no, downward monotone restrictor -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
No dog barked.
====
No black dog barked.
>>> 1
}

# n05: no, instance -- new
{
No dog barked.
Fido is a dog.
====
Fido barked.
>>> -1
}

# n06: no, restrictor not upward monotone -- after MED (Yanaka et al. 2019)
{
No black dog barked.
====
No dog barked.
>>> 0
}

# n07: no with relative clause, instance -- new
{
No student who read the article failed the exam.
Ann is a student who read the article.
====
Ann did not fail the exam.
>>> 1
}

# n08: no with relative clause, instance negated -- new
{
No student who read the article failed the exam.
Ann is a student who read the article.
====
Ann failed the exam.
>>> -1
}

# n09: no with relative clause, restrictor not met -- new
{
No student who read the article failed the exam.
Ann is a student.
====
Ann did not fail the exam.
>>> 0
}

# n10: conditional, modus ponens -- new
{
If the bus arrived, Sue left the station.
The bus arrived.
====
Sue left the station.
>>> 1
}

# n11: conditional, modus tollens as contradiction -- new
{
If the bus arrived, Sue left the station.
Sue did not leave the station.
====
The bus arrived.
>>> -1
}

# n12: conditional, denying the antecedent -- new
{
If the bus arrived, Sue left the station.
The bus did not arrive.
====
Sue left the station.
>>> 0
}

# n13: conditional, affirming the consequent -- new
{
If the bus arrived, Sue left the station.
Sue left the station.
====
The bus arrived.
>>> 0
}

# n14: negated predicate nominal -- new
{
Kim is not a doctor.
====
Kim is a doctor.
>>> -1
}

# n15: negated predicate nominal vs. other property -- new
{
Kim is not a doctor.
====
Kim is a lawyer.
>>> 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comparatives and equatives
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# c01: comparative, transitivity -- after FraCaS 6.x (comparatives)
{
Ann is taller than Bill.
Bill is taller than Carl.
====
Ann is taller than Carl.
>>> 1
}

# c02: comparative, asymmetry -- after FraCaS 6.x (comparatives)
{
Ann is taller than Bill.
====
Bill is taller than Ann.
>>> -1
}

# c03: comparative does not entail the positive -- after FraCaS 6.x (comparatives)
{
Ann is taller than Bill.
====
Ann is tall.
>>> 0
}

# c04: comparative, positive passes upward -- new
{
Ann is taller than Bill.
Bill is tall.
====
Ann is tall.
>>> 1
}

# c05: comparative, positive does not pass downward -- new
{
Ann is taller than Bill.
Ann is tall.
====
Bill is tall.
>>> 0
}

# c06: equative vs. comparative -- after FraCaS 6.x (comparatives)
{
Ann is as old as Bill.
====
Bill is older than Ann.
>>> -1
}

# c07: equative and comparative, transitivity -- new
{
Ann is as old as Bill.
Bill is older than Carl.
====
Ann is older than Carl.
>>> 1
}

# c08: negated equative, no positive -- new
{
Ann is not as old as Bill.
====
Bill is old.
>>> 0
}

# c09: negated equative as reversed comparative -- new
{
Ann is not as old as Bill.
====
Bill is older than Ann.
>>> 1
}

# c10: negated equative vs. comparative -- new
{
Ann is not as old as Bill.
====
Ann is older than Bill.
>>> -1
}

# c11: comparative vs. equative, definites -- new
{
The truck is heavier than the van.
====
The van is as heavy as the truck.
>>> -1
}

# c12: comparative with universal standard -- after FraCaS 6.x (comparatives)
{
Ann is older than every student in the class.
Bill is a student in the class.
====
Ann is older than Bill.
>>> 1
}

# c13: comparative with universal standard, no positive -- after FraCaS 6.x (comparatives)
{
Ann is older than every student in the class.
====
Ann is old.
>>> 0
}

# c14: comparative with universal standard, reversed -- new
{
Ann is older than every student in the class.
Bill is a student in the class.
====
Bill is older than Ann.
>>> -1
}

# c15: attributive comparative -- new
{
Ann is a faster swimmer than Bill.
====
Ann is a swimmer.
>>> 1
}

# c16: comparative, chain, converse -- new
{
The red bike is cheaper than the blue bike.
The blue bike is cheaper than the green bike.
====
The green bike is cheaper than the red bike.
>>> -1
}

# c17: comparative and equative, chain -- new
{
The red bike is cheaper than the blue bike.
The blue bike is as cheap as the green bike.
====
The green bike is cheaper than the red bike.
>>> -1
}

# c18: comparative without positive, definites -- new
{
The red bike is cheaper than the blue bike.
====
The red bike is cheap.
>>> 0
}

# c19: equative, transitivity -- new
{
The red bike is as cheap as the blue bike.
The blue bike is as cheap as the green bike.
====
The red bike is as cheap as the green bike.
>>> 1
}

# c20: equative is "at least as" -- after FraCaS 6.x (comparatives)
{
The red bike is as cheap as the blue bike.
====
The red bike is cheaper than the blue bike.
>>> 0
}

# c21: comparative chain through a universal, converse -- new
{
Ann is stronger than Bill.
Bill is stronger than every boxer in the gym.
Carl is a boxer in the gym.
====
Carl is stronger than Ann.
>>> -1
}

# c22: comparative chain, cycle -- new
{
Ann is younger than Bill.
Bill is younger than Carl.
====
Carl is younger than Ann.
>>> -1
}

# c23: comparative, unrelated third -- new
{
Ann is younger than Bill.
====
Ann is younger than Carl.
>>> 0
}

# c24: equative passes the positive upward -- new
{
The van is as heavy as the truck.
The truck is heavy.
====
The van is not heavy.
>>> -1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relative clauses
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# r01: subject relative in a definite -- new
{
The student who wrote the paper won a prize.
====
A student won a prize.
>>> 1
}

# r02: indefinite vs. universal with relative clause -- new
{
A student who wrote a paper won a prize.
====
Every student who wrote a paper won a prize.
>>> 0
}

# r03: indefinite with relative clause vs. no -- new
{
A student who wrote a paper won a prize.
====
No student won a prize.
>>> -1
}

# r04: object relative -- new
{
The professor praised the student that Sue hired.
====
Sue hired a student.
>>> 1
}

# r05: object relative, roles not shared -- new
{
The professor praised the student that Sue hired.
====
The professor hired a student.
>>> 0
}

# r06: object relative vs. agentless passive -- new
{
The professor praised the student that Sue hired.
====
No student was hired.
>>> -1
}

# r07: negation inside a relative clause -- new
{
Every guest who did not bring a present left.
Sue is a guest.
Sue did not bring a present.
====
Sue left.
>>> 1
}

# r08: relative clause, instance negated -- new
{
Every guest who brought a present danced.
Sue is a guest who brought a present.
====
Sue did not dance.
>>> -1
}

# r09: relative clause, converse -- new
{
Every guest who brought a present danced.
Sue is a guest who danced.
====
Sue brought a present.
>>> 0
}

# r10: stacked relative clauses, embedded content -- after "This is the house that Jack built"
{
The dog that chased the cat that ate the mouse barked.
====
A cat ate a mouse.
>>> 1
}

# r11: stacked relative clauses, roles not shared -- new
{
The dog that chased the cat that ate the mouse barked.
====
A dog chased a mouse.
>>> 0
}

# r12: stacked relative clauses vs. no -- new
{
The dog that chased the cat that ate the mouse barked.
====
No dog barked.
>>> -1
}

# r13: relative clause with a locative -- new
{
Kim met a doctor who lives in Paris.
====
A doctor lives in Paris.
>>> 1
}

# r14: relative clause with a locative, subject not shared -- new
{
Kim met a doctor who lives in Paris.
====
Kim lives in Paris.
>>> 0
}

# r15: predicative relative clause vs. no -- new
{
A woman who is a lawyer bought the house.
====
No lawyer bought a house.
>>> -1
}

# r16: relative clause with a locative, head negated -- new
{
Kim met a doctor who lives in Paris.
====
Kim did not meet a doctor.
>>> -1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Presupposition
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# p01: definite under negation projects -- after van der Sandt (1992)
{
Sue did not meet the man who painted the portrait.
====
A man painted a portrait.
>>> 1
}

# p02: definite under negation, content contradicted -- new
{
Sue did not meet the man who painted the portrait.
====
No man painted a portrait.
>>> -1
}

# p03: definite under negation, main content not asserted -- new
{
Sue did not meet the man who painted the portrait.
====
Sue met a man.
>>> 0
}

# p04: definite in a conditional antecedent projects -- after Karttunen (1973)
{
If the guide arrived, the tourists smiled.
====
There is a guide.
>>> 1
}

# p05: presupposition filtered in a conditional -- after Karttunen (1973)
{
If Bill wrote a novel, the novel is long.
====
Bill wrote a novel.
>>> 0
}

# p06: presupposition from the scope of no projects -- after van der Sandt (1992)
{
No student read the article.
====
There is an article.
>>> 1
}

# p07: definite with an of-PP -- new
{
The director of the museum resigned.
====
There is a director of the museum.
>>> 1
}

# p08: definite with an of-PP vs. no -- new
{
The director of the museum resigned.
====
No director resigned.
>>> -1
}

# p09: definite with an of-PP, other description -- new
{
The director of the museum resigned.
====
The director of the zoo resigned.
>>> 0
}

# p10: definite with object relative under negation -- new
{
Kim did not find the key that Sue lost.
====
Sue lost a key.
>>> 1
}

# p11: definite with object relative under negation, main content -- new
{
Kim did not find the key that Sue lost.
====
Kim found a key.
>>> 0
}

# p12: definite with object relative under negation, presupposition denied -- new
{
Kim did not find the key that Sue lost.
====
Sue did not lose a key.
>>> -1
}

# p13: definite in the scope of every projects, existence denied -- after van der Sandt (1992)
{
Every guest greeted the host.
====
There is no host.
>>> -1
}

# p14: nested definites under negation project, content denied -- new
{
Kim did not visit the castle that the king built.
====
The king did not build a castle.
>>> -1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Active/passive alternation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# a01: active to passive -- in the style of SICK (Marelli et al. 2014)
{
The board rejected the proposal.
====
The proposal was rejected by the board.
>>> 1
}

# a02: passive drops the agent -- new
{
The proposal was rejected by the board.
====
The proposal was rejected.
>>> 1
}

# a03: agentless passive does not give the agent -- new
{
The proposal was rejected.
====
The board rejected the proposal.
>>> 0
}

# a04: passive vs. negated active -- new
{
The proposal was rejected by the board.
====
The board did not reject the proposal.
>>> -1
}

# a05: active vs. passive with reversed roles -- new
{
The editor criticized the author.
====
The editor was criticized by the author.
>>> 0
}

# a06: active vs. passive with the same roles -- new
{
The editor criticized the author.
====
The author was criticized by the editor.
>>> 1
}

# a07: passive to active, indefinites -- new
{
The thief was caught by a policeman.
====
A policeman caught a thief.
>>> 1
}

# a08: passive vs. no -- new
{
The thief was caught by a policeman.
====
No policeman caught the thief.
>>> -1
}

# a09: passive, roles reversed -- new
{
The thief was caught by a policeman.
====
The thief caught a policeman.
>>> 0
}

# a10: passive with a relative clause in the subject -- new
{
The necklace that the thief stole was found by the maid.
====
The maid found a necklace.
>>> 1
}

# a11: passive with a relative clause in the subject, roles not shared -- new
{
The necklace that the thief stole was found by the maid.
====
The thief found a necklace.
>>> 0
}

# a12: passive with a relative clause in the subject vs. no -- new
{
The necklace that the thief stole was found by the maid.
====
No necklace was found.
>>> -1
}

# a13: negated passive to negated active -- new
{
The letter was not signed by the manager.
====
The manager did not sign the letter.
>>> 1
}

# a14: negated passive vs. active -- new
{
The letter was not signed by the manager.
====
The manager signed the letter.
>>> -1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dative alternation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# d01: double object to prepositional dative -- after Levin (1993) 2.1
{
The waiter brought the guest a menu.
====
The waiter brought a menu to the guest.
>>> 1
}

# d02: double object to recipient passive -- after Levin (1993) 2.1
{
The museum offered the artist a contract.
====
The artist was offered a contract.
>>> 1
}

# d03: double object, roles reversed -- new
{
The museum offered the artist a contract.
====
The artist offered the museum a contract.
>>> 0
}

# d04: prepositional dative vs. negated double object -- new
{
The museum offered a contract to the artist.
====
The museum did not offer the artist a contract.
>>> -1
}

# d05: offering is not receiving -- new
{
The museum offered the artist a contract.
====
The artist received a contract.
>>> 0
}

# d06: double object to prepositional dative, adjective dropped -- new
{
Bill sold Sue an old car.
====
Bill sold a car to Sue.
>>> 1
}

# d07: prepositional dative does not give the adjective -- new
{
Bill sold a car to Sue.
====
Bill sold Sue an old car.
>>> 0
}

# d08: double object vs. negated recipient passive -- new
{
Bill sold Sue an old car.
====
Sue was not sold a car by Bill.
>>> -1
}

# d09: double object with universal recipient -- new
{
The teacher handed every student an exam.
Ann is a student.
====
The teacher handed an exam to Ann.
>>> 1
}

# d10: double object with universal recipient, instance negated -- new
{
The teacher handed every student an exam.
Ann is a student.
====
The teacher did not hand Ann an exam.
>>> -1
}

# d11: double object vs. negated prepositional passive with agent -- new
{
Sue showed the visitor the garden.
====
The garden was not shown to the visitor by Sue.
>>> -1
}

# d12: double object, roles reversed -- new
{
Sue showed the visitor the garden.
====
The visitor showed Sue the garden.
>>> 0
}

# d13: recipient passive vs. negated double object -- new
{
The visitor was shown the garden by Sue.
====
Sue did not show the visitor the garden.
>>> -1
}

# d14: prepositional dative, roles reversed -- new
{
Kim sent a letter to Sue.
====
Sue sent a letter to Kim.
>>> 0
}

