# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ambiguous premises. Each group shares a premise; every reading is needed by one item.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# s01: scope (every > a and a > every), anaphora to the object -- after May (1985) [reading: a > every, the professor bound to it]
{
Every student in the class admires a professor.
The professor won a prize.
====
Every student in the class admires a professor who won a prize.
>>> 1
}

# s02: scope -- after May (1985) [reading: every > a]
{
Every student in the class admires a professor.
====
There is a professor who every student in the class admires.
>>> 0
}

# s03: scope, instance -- after May (1985) [both readings]
{
Every student in the class admires a professor.
Ann is a student in the class.
====
Ann does not admire a professor.
>>> -1
}

# s04: negation and an indefinite with a relative clause [reading: a > not]
{
Kim did not read a book that Sue recommended.
====
Sue recommended a book.
>>> 1
}

# s05: negation and an indefinite with a relative clause [reading: not > a]
{
Kim did not read a book that Sue recommended.
Sue recommended Emma.
Emma is a book.
====
Kim read Emma.
>>> -1
}

# s06: negation and an indefinite with a relative clause [reading: a > not]
{
Kim did not read a book that Sue recommended.
====
Kim read a book that Sue recommended.
>>> 0
}

# s07: pronoun with two antecedents of the same gender [reading: he = Bill]
{
Bill visited Carl.
He was exhausted.
====
Bill was exhausted.
>>> 1
}

# s08: pronoun with two antecedents of the same gender [reading: he = Carl]
{
Bill visited Carl.
He was exhausted.
====
Carl was exhausted.
>>> 1
}

# s09: pronoun with two antecedents of the same gender [reading: he = Bill]
{
Bill visited Carl.
He was exhausted.
====
Bill was not exhausted.
>>> -1
}

# s10: pronoun with two antecedents of the same gender [reading: he = Bill]
{
Bill visited Carl.
He was exhausted.
====
Carl was not exhausted.
>>> 0
}

# s11: PP attachment -- after the classic telescope example [reading: NP attachment]
{
Kim photographed the woman with the camera.
====
A woman with a camera was photographed by Kim.
>>> 1
}

# s12: PP attachment -- after the classic telescope example [reading: VP attachment]
{
Kim photographed the woman with the camera.
====
No woman with a camera was photographed by Kim.
>>> 0
}

# s13: scope in the double object construction [reading: every > a]
{
The teacher gave every student a book.
====
There is a book that the teacher gave every student.
>>> 0
}

# s14: scope in the double object construction, anaphora to the theme [reading: a > every, the book bound to it]
{
The teacher gave every student a book.
The book was long.
====
The teacher gave every student a long book.
>>> 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Longer sentences
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# l01: long, stacked modifiers -- new
{
The chef who cooked the soup that every guest praised received a prize.
